{"qid":"Q631210","names":["Nogai Horde","Small Horde"],"title":"Nogai Horde","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Nogai Horde was a nomadic Turkic-Mongol confederation that emerged from the dissolution of the Golden Horde in the late 14th century, named after the influential general Nogai Khan; it consolidated as an independent power on the Pontic-Caspian steppe between the Volga and Ural rivers around 1500 under the biy Musa. At its height in the early 16th century the Horde controlled vast pastures from the Caspian to the Dnieper, fielded formidable cavalry, and intervened in the politics of the Kazan and Astrakhan khanates. Internal succession conflicts, combined with Russian expansion under Ivan IV and the westward migration of the Kalmyks after 1608, shattered the confederation; by the 1630s most Nogai groups had been pushed westward or absorbed, with the eastern pastures lost to the Kalmyks.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Nogai territorial extents are derived from Russian and Ottoman diplomatic records and traveler accounts describing general zones of migration and tribute; as a nomadic confederation, precise boundaries cannot be established, and the mapped extent is a best estimate of seasonal range rather than a controlled territory.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nogai Horde","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nogai_Horde"}],"britannica":"place/Nogay-Steppe","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Nogai Horde","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Nogai Khan","nativeName":"Ногай","roman":"Nogay","start":1262,"end":1299,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Isa","nativeN